{"custom_id":"s-ap_calculus_bc-m04-k0","ok":true,"model":"claude-opus-5-5","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfs4uCpG6hjDdngRac3LH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"**The greatest speed is 8.642 (choice index 1), reached at the endpoint t = 4.**\n\n1. The speed is the length of the velocity vector: s(t) = √(4t² + 25cos²t).\n2. Use the squared speed, f(t) = 4t² + 25cos²t, because it is largest where the speed is largest.\n3. Its derivative is f′(t) = 8t − 25 sin(2t). Check the candidates:\n   - At t = 0: f = 25, so the speed is 5.\n   - At t = π: f = 4π² + 25 ≈ 64.48, so the speed is about 8.03.\n   - Between π and 4, f′ stays positive. For example, at t = 3.5, f′ = 28 − 25 sin 7 ≈ 28 − 16.4 > 0. So the speed is still increasing as t approaches 4.\n   - At t = 4: cos 4 ≈ −0.6536, so f ≈ 64 + 25(0.4272) ≈ 74.68, and the speed is √74.68 ≈ 8.642.\n\nI didn't solve f′(t) = 0 exactly. Any interior critical points lie before π, and the speed there must be below the endpoint value, which is the largest of the values above.\n\nThe item is not flawed."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YJs48wnRFmMEwJLzGgSe8D","name":"submit_solution","input":{"answers":[{"item":"1","choice_index":1,"final_answer":"8.642","brief_reasoning":"Speed^2=4t^2+25cos^2 t; increasing near end, max at t=4: sqrt(64+25cos^2 4)=8.642","ambiguous_or_flawed":false}]},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":258,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":568,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":791,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":220},"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null},"usage":{"input_tokens":258,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":568,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_to